course: everyday
lesson: 306
title: Breakfast, the school day and honest homework
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A school day runs smoothly when everyone knows the plan: eat something before you go, answer the register, follow the timetable and walk calmly in the corridors.
explain: The register or roll call is not just a list: in a fire or other emergency, the school must know exactly which children are in the building.
explain: Example: a girl in Wellington hears the earthquake drill alarm. She drops to the floor, crawls under her desk and holds on to its leg until the teacher says the shaking (or the drill) is over.
explain: If the adult who usually collects you does not come, go back to the school office; the school will phone your family and keep you safe until someone arrives.
explain: Homework should be your own work: if you find facts on the internet, write them in your own words and say where you found them.
fact: Eat something simple before school, such as toast, fruit or cereal; the brain needs food to concentrate.
fact: Skipping breakfast can make you tired or grumpy by mid-morning.
fact: Answer the register clearly, for example «here» or «present», because the school must know who is in the building in an emergency.
fact: Check the timetable the night before so you bring the right things and know where to be.
fact: In the corridor, walk on the correct side, do not run and keep your voice low, because running in crowds causes collisions and falls.
fact: In an earthquake, drop, take cover under a sturdy desk and hold on until the shaking stops; falling objects cause most injuries.
fact: If nobody comes to pick you up, go back into the school office and tell a teacher; never walk off or accept a lift from someone you were not told about.
fact: Follow the school's rule about phones, usually switched off in your bag or handed in, because phones distract from lessons.
fact: Put books, pencils and papers back at the end of the lesson, so you can find things and the room stays safe.
fact: If a classmate's noise stops you concentrating, ask kindly first and tell the teacher if it continues.
fact: Copying text and passing it off as your own is plagiarism, a kind of cheating; write in your own words and say where the facts came from.
exercise: A boy in Ohio is late and wants to skip breakfast. What is best? | Eat something quick, like a banana or a slice of toast | Skip it and eat a double helping at lunch to catch up on the energy | Drink a fizzy drink on the way | Eat a bag of sweets at morning break
exercise: Why does eating breakfast help at school? | The brain needs food to concentrate | It makes you grow a little taller before lunchtime | Breakfast stops you catching any colds | Teachers check what you ate that morning
exercise: The teacher calls your name for the register. What should you do? | Answer clearly, «here» or «present» | Wave silently from the back so you don't disturb anyone | Answer for your absent friend too | Wait until she calls it twice
exercise: Why does the school take the register every day? | So it knows exactly who is in the building in an emergency | So it can give prizes at the end of term to the children who are never away | So the teacher can practise reading names | So children learn to spell their names
exercise: Tomorrow is Tuesday, and on Tuesdays you have PE and music. When should you check your timetable? | The night before, to pack the right things | On the bus in the morning | Only on the morning itself, when the teacher asks for your kit | Once at the start of the year
exercise: The corridor is crowded at lunchtime. How should you move? | Walk on the correct side and keep your voice low | Run on the left so you get through the crowd faster than everyone else | Walk in the middle so everyone sees you | Push gently past people who are slow
exercise: During an earthquake drill in California, what should you do? | Drop, cover under your desk and hold on | Run outside as fast as you can, away from the walls | Stand in the middle of the room | Hide behind the classroom window
exercise: Why do you take cover under a sturdy desk in an earthquake? | Falling objects cause most injuries and the desk protects your head | Desks are screwed to the ground, so the floor under them does not shake at all | Desks are fixed to the ground and cannot move | It keeps you warm until help arrives
exercise: Your mum usually picks you up, but today nobody has come after school. What should you do? | Go back into the school office and tell a teacher | Start walking home on your own along the usual way you drive | Accept a lift from a friendly stranger | Wait alone at the gate until dark
exercise: A neighbour you know a little offers you a lift home, but your parents did not tell you about this. What is right? | Say no and go back to the school office | Get in because you know the neighbour well | Get in if the neighbour has a nice new car | Get in if the neighbour says your mum asked
exercise: Your school says phones must stay switched off in bags. You want to check a game in the lesson. What should you do? | Keep it switched off, as the rule says | Check it quickly under the desk while the teacher is busy | Leave it on silent in your pocket | Ask a friend to check it for you
exercise: The lesson ends and your table is covered in pencils and papers. What should you do? | Put everything back where it belongs | Leave it for the next class to tidy | Push it all on the floor | Stuff it all into your coat pocket
exercise: A classmate keeps tapping a pencil loudly and you cannot concentrate. What is the best first step? | Ask them kindly to stop | Grab the pencil from them | Shout at them across the room | Tap louder to drown it out
exercise: You find a great paragraph about volcanoes online for your homework. What is honest? | Write the facts in your own words and say where you found them | Copy the paragraph exactly, because the website already says it best, and put your name on it | Change one or two words and hand it in | Copy it but in your best handwriting
exercise: What is plagiarism? | Passing off someone else's words or work as your own | Writing a report about a famous person's life | Using a dictionary to check spelling | Asking a librarian for a good book
test: A girl in Leeds feels very tired and grumpy by eleven o'clock every school day. What might help most? | Eating something simple for breakfast | Going to bed later so she sleeps in deeper | Running in the corridors at break | Skipping lunch as well
test: A fire alarm rings and the class goes outside. Why does the teacher read the register on the field? | To check that every child who was in school is safely out | To see who is wearing the right school uniform and shoes | To choose a child to lead the line | To practise the names before next year
test: A boy in Texas runs round a corner in the corridor and crashes into a younger child carrying a tray. Which rule would have prevented this? | Walk, do not run, in corridors | Always carry a tray with both hands | Keep your voice low at lunchtime | Always leave the classroom last of all
test: A boy in Seattle feels the classroom start to shake hard. Which is the right action? | Get under his desk, cover his head and hold on | Run down the stairs and out of the building straight away | Stand in the doorway on his own | Go and look out of the window
test: A car you do not know stops at the school gate and the driver says, «Your dad sent me, hop in.» Nobody told you about this. What do you do? | Go back into the school and tell a teacher | Get in because he knows your dad | Get in if he knows your name | Ask him to wait while you finish a game
test: Why do many schools keep phones off during lessons? | Phones distract from learning, and are meant for emergencies only | Phones break easily if they are used indoors, near radiators and whiteboards | Phones make classroom lights flicker | Teachers want to read the messages
test: A girl copies a whole page from a website for her project and says she wrote it. What has she done? | Plagiarised, which is a kind of cheating | Researched it well, which is fine for a project | Summarised it fairly | Quoted it correctly
test: Why is a tidy table useful at the end of a lesson? | You find things quickly and nobody trips over things left about | It makes the lesson finish early, so everyone gets a longer break | It lets the teacher skip marking | It keeps the pencils sharp
test: A classmate keeps chatting while you try to read. You asked kindly, but it went on. What next? | Tell the teacher calmly | Move to another classroom | Throw a rubber at them to stop it | Start chatting loudly too
test: You forgot to check your timetable and arrive without your swimming kit. What would have prevented this? | Checking the timetable the night before | Getting up earlier in the morning | Asking a friend to bring two kits | Taking every kit to school every day
test: no time for brekky, ill eat at lunch | Eat something quick, your brain needs food to focus | Fine, skipping breakfast helps you focus coz your tummy's empty | Fine, just drink a fizzy drink | Fine, eat double at lunch
test: nobodys come to get me after school n everyone's gone | Go back to the office and tell a teacher | Walk home by yourself | Get a lift from a friend's older cousin | Wait outside the gate till dark
test: shaking!! classroom's shaking, earthquake | Drop, take cover under a desk and hold on | Run out into the corridor and down the stairs | Stand in the middle of the room | Hide by the windows
test: copied the dino bit off a website for homework, thats fine yeah | No, write it in your own words and say where it came from | Yes, the website said it best | Yes, if you change one word | Only if the teacher doesn't check, they never look it up online anyway
explain: A boy in England skipped breakfast every day; by mid-morning he felt dizzy and grumpy, and once he fainted in PE.
explain: When a strong earthquake hit Christchurch in 2011, children at school dropped under their desks and held on as they had practised, and many stayed safe while things fell.
fact: Many people hurt in earthquakes are hit by falling things or cut by glass while trying to run.
fact: Going too long without food can make children faint, especially when they exercise.
test: ground shaking in class, run outside? | Drop, cover under the desk and hold on | Run for the classroom door as fast as you can | Stand by the window to see what is happening | Climb up on top of the desk and hold tight
test: no time for brekkie, its fine | Eat something small, or you may feel faint | It's fine, you'll just eat more at lunch | Drink a fizzy drink instead | Skip it, it makes you lighter
test: nobody came to pick me up at school | Go back to the office and tell a teacher | Walk home alone along the way you usually drive | Wait outside the gate on your own until dark | Go with anyone who offers to give you a lift
test: did u hear a girl skipped breakfast and then did PE and "fainted flat on the floor, ambulance came". lesson? | Eat something before school; the body needs food | Breakfast doesn't matter for kids | Only eat if you're really hungry, never just coz it's morning | Fainting is normal in PE
test: my mate says nobody picked up a kid and he "walked home 5 miles in the dark and a stranger gave him a lift". what's the real lesson | If nobody comes, go back to the office and tell a teacher | Walk home if no one comes, just keep to the main roads | Accept a lift from anyone kind | Wait at the gate till dark
test: kids say the girl who got caught copying an essay "is a cheat and always has been". pass it on? | No, don't spread it; just write in your own words yourself | Yes, she's a cheat | Yes, and tell her parents | Yes, everyone should know
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required during an earthquake within a classroom? | Persons shall drop, take cover beneath a sturdy desk and hold on | Persons shall run out of doors at once, by the nearest staircase | Persons shall stand in the window frames | Persons shall remain seated without cover
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pupil answers the register clearly. | The school must know who is in the building in an emergency | It improves the pupil's marks for speaking and listening at the end of the year | The teacher is otherwise unpaid | It decides the order of lunch
exam: It is given that a pupil has copied text and presented it as their own. Select the term consistent with the established facts. | Plagiarism, a kind of cheating | Research, a kind of study | Quotation, a form of reference | Summary, a form of note-taking
exam: State the purpose of checking the timetable on the night before school. | To bring the right things and know where to be | To decide which of the next day's lessons to miss | To count the hours until the holidays | To choose one's teacher for the day
